<.shell current={:layout}>
  <h1>Layout</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every page in an area has the area's pages down the left, starts with the same header, and stops content at a readable width. A page for one task in hand is a narrow page.
  </p>

  <h2>Page header</h2>
  <p>
    On a record's page, breadcrumbs, then the title. A section's own page starts with its title.
  </p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.breadcrumbs>
      <:item label="Activities" path="#" />
      <:item label="25-0412" />
    </.breadcrumbs>
    <h1 class="title">Missing hiker, Stawamus Chief</h1>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    In the app: <code>&lt;.breadcrumbs&gt;</code>, then an <code>h1</code>
    with <code>class="title"</code>, which keeps 16px under it. A page's actions sit with its content, not in the header.
  </p>

  <h2>A heading with an action</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href={page_path(:heading_row)}>heading row</a>: the heading on the left, its note and
    action on the right.
  </p>

  <h2>A picture beside its words</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <div class="media">
      <img src={~p"/images/sarduty-logo.svg"} alt="" class="logo-md" />
      <div>
        <p class="subheading mb-0">Squamish SAR</p>
        <p class="hint mb-0">BC Search and Rescue Association</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    <code>.media</code>: a logo or photo, then its words, centred. Photos and logos use <code>.photo</code>, <code>.photo-sm</code>, and <code>.logo-md</code>, or a
    size from the <a href={page_path(:spacing)}>spacing</a> scale, such as <code>size-8</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Page width</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The <a href={page_path(:side_nav)}>side navigation</a>
      takes 200px on the left of every page in an area, and content fills the rest. Account settings has no side navigation.
    </li>
    <li>Content fills up to 1280px; tables use the full width.</li>
    <li>
      Text, forms, and maps stop at 640px with <code>.measure</code>, so lines stay readable. An article uses <code>max-w-prose</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A record's page puts its <a href={page_path(:detail_list)}>details</a>
      in a sidebar beside the main
      content: <code>.content-wrapper</code>, with <code>.content-1/3</code>
      and <code>.content-2/3</code>. Its <a href={page_path(:tabs)}>tabs</a>
      sit under the title, above both columns.
    </li>
    <li>
      Desktop first. On a phone, layouts stack and wide tables scroll sideways. A page for one task done with a phone in hand is a
      <a href="#narrow-pages">narrow page</a>
      instead.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="narrow-pages">Narrow pages</h2>
  <p>
    A page for one task done with a phone or iPad in hand, such as logging in, taking attendance, or scanning kit, is one column, centred, at every width. A big screen adds margin, not width. There is no side navigation, and the
    <a href={page_path(:top_bar)}>top bar</a>
    is the compact one at every width, with its ends lined up with the column.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Form, 28rem (448px):</strong>
      one short form, such as Log in, the login code, or Sign up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tool, 48rem (768px):</strong>
      a hand-held tool with a list, such as Take attendance or a scanner.
    </li>
    <li>
      The team's name is in the bar, never in the page. Logged out, the bar has nothing on the left or right. Logged in, Menu holds the area's pages, so the page is never a dead end. There is no search: the page is for one task, and Menu is the way out.
    </li>
    <li>
      A page people move between stays in its area with side navigation, even when they mostly open it on a phone, such as Your membership.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar size={:form} current_path="/login" />
    <div class={["m-auto px-2 py-6", Web.Layouts.column_width(:form)]}>
      <h1 class="title">Log in</h1>
      <.button variant={:success}>Send me a code</.button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar
      size={:tool}
      current_user={sample_user()}
      current_team={sample_team()}
      current_member={sample_member()}
      team_admin?={true}
      managed_teams={sample_managed_teams()}
      member_logins={sample_member_logins()}
      current_path="/teams/squamish/activities"
    />
    <div class={["m-auto px-2 py-6", Web.Layouts.column_width(:tool)]}>
      <h1 class="title">Take attendance</h1>
      <p class="mb-0">Scan each member's ID card as they arrive.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    In the app: <code>use Web, :live_view_narrow_layout</code>
    for a form, or <code>use Web, {"{:live_view_narrow_layout, size: :tool}"}</code>
    for a tool. The bar's <code>site-bar-compact</code>
    class shows it as on a phone. The ID card and letter checks use their own verify layout, so they don't look like the app.
  </p>
</.shell>
